<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · 1862 · Americas</p><h1 class="sg-title">Battle Cry of Freedom</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A rousing Union marching song of the American Civil War, written in 1862, that called for the Union and for freedom.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>George Frederick Root</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>George Frederick Root</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the Union in the American Civil War</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>United States (the Union)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1862</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words and music by George Frederick Root (1862; he died in 1895); Confederate version by H. L. Schreiner and W. H. Barnes)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;Battle Cry of Freedom&quot;, also known as &quot;Rally 'Round the Flag&quot;, was written in 1862, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, by the composer George Frederick Root (1820–1895). It was a patriotic song for the causes of Unionism and abolitionism, and it spread fast: over 700,000 copies of the sheet music are estimated to have circulated, and the publisher ran fourteen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> presses at once without keeping up with demand.</p><p>It was a favourite of Union soldiers. Henry Stone, a veteran, recalled in the late 1880s how a glee club from Chicago brought the new song to his camp, where it &quot;ran through the camp like wildfire&quot; and put as much spirit into the army as a victory; it was heard day and night at every camp fire. The historian Christian L. McWhirter explains its success by its even-handedness: its definition of the Northern cause is open-ended, with anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> feeling there for those who looked for it but not so pronounced as to offend those who fought only for the Union. The chorus endorses a perpetual Union and rejects secession, but the cry it raises is one of freedom, which, given Root's political beliefs and other activities, he clearly meant to suggest some degree of abolitionism.</p><p>The song was so popular that the composer H. L. Schreiner and the lyricist W. H. Barnes adapted it for the Confederacy. A modified Union version served as the campaign song of the Lincoln–Johnson ticket in the presidential election of 1864, and again after the war, for Garfield in 1880. Louis Moreau Gottschalk, a friend of Root's, wrote in his diary that he thought &quot;it should be our national anthem&quot;, and in 1863 made it the basis of his concert paraphrase for solo piano, &quot;Le Cri de délivrance&quot;, opus 55, dedicated to Root. Jane Curry Blaikie Hoge printed an extended version in The Boys in Blue (1867); Walter Van Brunt recorded the Union version on an Edison Blue Amberol cylinder in 1916; and Charles Ives quoted the song in several of his works, among them his own patriotic song &quot;They Are There&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A rallying song for the Union: its men are called to gather around the flag, shouting the battle cry of freedom, for a Union that must last and against those who would break it. The song looks to the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> without making it the whole cause. The mood is stirring and confident.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our flag is proudly floating on the land and on the main</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle cry of freedom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Beneath it oft we've conquer'd and will conquer oft again</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle cry of freedom</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our Dixie forever, she's never at a loss</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Down with the eagle and up with the cross</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll rally 'round the bonny flag, we'll rally once again</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shouting the battle cry of freedom</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our gallant boys have marched to the rolling of the drum</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle-cry of freedom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And leader in charge cry, &quot;come boys come&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle-cry of freedom</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our Dixie forever, she's never at a loss</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Down with the eagle and up with the cross</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll rally 'round the bonny flag, we'll rally once again</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shouting the battle cry of freedom</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>They have laid down their lives on the bloody battle-field</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle-cry of freedom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Their motto is resistance - &quot;To tyrants we'll not yield&quot;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle-cry of freedom</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our Dixie forever, she's never at a loss</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Down with the eagle and up with the cross</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll rally 'round the bonny flag, we'll rally once again</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shouting the battle cry of freedom</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>While our boys have responded and to the field have gone</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle-cry of freedom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Oar noble women also have aided them at home</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle-cry of freedom</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our women forever, God bless them, huzza!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With their smiles and favors, they aid us in the war</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In the tent and on the battle-field, the boys remember</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And cheer for the daughters of freedom</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Let the joyous sound re-echo o'er the land and o'er the sea</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle-cry of freedom</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our Southern sky is brightening and soon we will be free</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shout, shout the battle-cry of freedom</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our Dixie forever, she's never at a loss</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Down with the eagle and up with the cross</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We'll rally 'round the bonny flag, we'll rally once again</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Shouting the battle cry of freedom</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: George Frederick Root (1862). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Battle_Cry_of_Freedom" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
